    69	## Purpose
    70	
    71	A recurring question in this system is *how do I stop caring about something
    72	that may never answer*. A holder is granted something — a pause, a connection's
    73	server-side composition, a claim on shared work — and then dies, hangs, or
    74	partitions away without saying so. The grantor must eventually take the grant
    75	back: without detecting death, which is undetectable in general; without
    76	consensus, which an unreachable peer cannot give; and without a stuck state,
    77	which any switch left on becomes.
    78	
    79	Java Jini solved this class of problem with the lease. This document keeps its
    80	insight and rejects its design.
    81	
    82	The insight: a grant that lapses unless renewed inverts the problem. Instead of
    83	the grantor proving a holder dead, the holder must keep proving itself
    84	interested, and the absence of proof is the reclaim condition. Silence — the
    85	one thing a dead holder reliably produces — becomes the signal.
    86	
    87	The design rejected: a lease as a live object with methods, a synchronous
    88	remote `renew`, expiry as absolute time in the holder's milliseconds, and a
    89	background `LeaseRenewalManager` thread. These are one mistake wearing four
    90	faces — **meaning placed where it cannot be read.** A live object cannot travel
    91	a stream, be stored, replayed, or read by an interpreter that does not exist
    92	yet. A synchronous remote call is a host boundary worn as a function call. An
    93	absolute expiration binds two clocks that share nothing — and it was always a
    94	fiction: only the grantor can reclaim, so the moment was never anyone's but the
    95	grantor's, and Jini merely disguised that. A background renewer manufactures
    96	precisely the false liveness the lease exists to end.
