    98	## A lease is facts, accreted
    99	
   100	No lease object exists. The record of a lease is its trace on a medium: at most
   101	one fact per negotiation status, any number of renewals, and — in the common
   102	case this vocabulary exists for — no terminal fact from the holder at all. Each
   103	fact is plain data on an ordinary stream. Keys live under `:dao.lease/…`, and
   104	maps are open in the usual way.
   105	
   106	Facts are classified by **two** dispatch keys, and a reader must switch on
   107	both: `:dao.lease/status` for the negotiation, `:dao.lease/event` for evidence.
   108	This mirrors the precedent's separation of its patch and trace streams by
   109	namespace.
   110	
   111	Following the precedent, the negotiation carries an explicit status. Each is
   112	authored exactly once, by the only party with authority to say it:
   113	
   114	| Fact      | `:dao.lease/status`   | Author  | Required beside `:dao.lease/lease`                                   |
   115	|-----------|-----------------------|---------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|
   116	| Proposal  | `:dao.lease/proposed` | holder  | `:dao.lease/subject`; `:dao.lease/duration` optional — an ask          |
   117	| Grant     | `:dao.lease/accepted` | grantor | `:dao.lease/subject`, `:dao.lease/duration`; `:dao.lease/max` optional |
   118	| Refusal   | `:dao.lease/rejected` | grantor | —                                                                     |
   119	| Release   | `:dao.lease/released` | holder  | —                                                                     |
   120	| Reclaim   | `:dao.lease/lapsed`   | grantor | — (`:dao.lease/cause` optional: silence, cap, policy)                 |
   121	
   122	`:dao.lease/subject` names **what is leased** — the resource, in whatever plain
   123	data the domain uses to denote it. The grant repeats it rather than referring
   124	back to a proposal, because a proposal may not exist and, where one does, a
   125	reader of the grant alone must still be able to say what was granted.
   126	
   127	`:dao.lease/lease` is the lease's identity: plain data, minted by whichever
   128	party authors the lease's first fact. It must be unique among **all** facts the
   129	judge will read on the judged medium, not merely among the minting party's own
   130	leases — two holders minting the same id would leave the grantor holding
   131	indistinguishable leases. Namespacing ids by author satisfies this, as does a
   132	medium whose attribution already separates them.
   133	
   134	`:dao.lease/duration` is a non-negative plain-data quantity carrying its unit.
   135	A grant whose duration is absent, negative, or malformed is not a grant and
   136	establishes nothing; a judge that reads one has read a defective fact, not a
   137	lease. The lapse comparison is strict: the guard fails when the measured
   138	interval **exceeds** the duration, so an interval exactly equal to it has not
   139	yet lapsed. `:dao.lease/max` optionally caps total consecutive tenure, and a
   140	`max` less than `duration` is likewise defective.
   141	
   142	**Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot renew itself
   143	into tenure by asserting one, and a replayed or delayed holder fact establishes
   144	nothing. A proposal may carry a desired duration; it is advisory, exactly as a
   145	patch's replacement block is advisory until the VM accepts it. A grantor may
   146	grant less than was asked and owes no explanation.
   147	
   148	**A proposal creates no state.** An unanswered proposal is not pending and is
   149	not queued — it is simply not a lease. Nothing exists until `:accepted`, and a
