   161	long to wait before proposing again is the holder's own policy. A proposal that
   162	was answered — accepted or refused — has spent its identity; renegotiating is a
   163	new proposal with a new lease id, since each status is authored exactly once.
   164	
   165	A grantor may also grant **unsolicited** — a lease whose first fact is
   166	`:accepted` — because the canonical case, a server leasing a per-connection
   167	composition, asks nobody first. Where a grant answers a proposal instead, it
   168	reaches the holder the same way every other fact does: the holder reads the
   169	medium the composition wired for it. This vocabulary provides no delivery
   170	mechanism and needs none.
   171	
   172	`:dao.lease/lapsed` is the reclaim record; its ordering with the act of
   173	reclaiming is specified under *Judging lapse*.
   174	
   175	There is no `:rolled-back`. The precedent's word is **renamed rather than
   176	omitted**, and the reason is connotation: rollback suggests undoing an
   177	application and restoring the world as if it had not happened, while a lapse
   178	ends a tenure whose entire past remains valid and undoes nothing. `:released`
   179	has no patch analogue at all, because an unapplied patch costs nothing while a
   180	holder done early saves the grantor up to one duration of waiting.
   181	
   182	## Renewal is evidence, not negotiation
   183	
   184	A renewal is the trace half:
   185	
   186	```clojure
   187	{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal
   188	 :dao.lease/lease <id>}
   189	```
   190	
   191	It carries no time and proposes nothing. It says one thing — *I still want
   192	this* — and the fact that it was appended, together with when it was observed,
   193	is its entire content. A renewal that asked for a new duration would be
   194	negotiating; renegotiation is a new proposal, not a fat renewal.
   195	
   196	**A renewal is a fact, not a claim on the future.** It obliges the grantor to
   197	nothing: the grantor re-judges at every judgment point and may stop honouring
   198	renewals at any moment without breaching anything, because the trace creates no
   199	obligations.
   200	
   201	**A renewal is never acknowledged**, so a holder never learns that its evidence
   202	was observed. Two rules follow for the holder, and both are its own discipline
   203	rather than anything the grantor enforces:
   204	
   205	- **Renew at a fraction of the duration** — half is the usual choice — and
   206	  treat the duration as a safety bound rather than a cadence.
   207	- **Stop acting at the bound.** A holder whose own clock has passed the
   208	  duration since its last renewal must cease acting on what it holds, whether
   209	  or not it has heard anything. Nothing in this vocabulary excludes a holder
   210	  that keeps working past its bound, and a paused agent that resumes after
   211	  lapse acts on work the grantor has already reclaimed and regranted.
   212	
   213	## Attribution is a composition duty
   214	
   215	A renewal is evidence **about its author**, and a grant establishes terms only
   216	because the grantor authored it. Both depend on the judge being able to tell
   217	who wrote a fact — and **DaoStream does not supply that.** Its Concurrency
   218	section contemplates multiple writers and leaves one-log-per-writer to
   219	interpretation; a descriptor carries no authorization; and the attribution a
   220	WebSocket boundary provides is composed envelope data, not a property of the
   221	medium.
   222	
   223	So the composition owes it. A judged medium must give the judge a basis for
   224	authorship — one medium per author, an author key the boundary adds, or
   225	transport attribution as the WebSocket boundary provides — and **a judged
   226	medium without one is a host assembly defect**, of the same rank as an
   227	ungoverned grantor. Absent attribution, "only grantor-authored facts establish
   228	terms" is unenforceable: any writer can append an `:accepted` fact naming
   229	itself and take tenure. This is not a theoretical corner. Of the three uses
   230	that justify this vocabulary, only the served connection has attribution by
   231	construction; the forwarder pause and the shared-work claim run over in-process
   232	media where nothing distinguishes writers unless a composition arranges it.
   233	
   234	## Lapse is a conclusion; reclaim is an act
   235	
   236	Nothing fires when a lease lapses. The grantor runs an interpreter that reads
   237	the medium through its own cursor, notes on its own clock when it observes each
   238	fact, and judges: when the interval since its last relevant observation exceeds
   239	the granted duration plus whatever tolerance this judge allows, the guard has
   240	failed.
   241	
   242	Anyone may reach that conclusion; only the possessor's conclusion has effect.
   243	The interpreter composed with the resource — the one that can actually close
   244	the socket, stop the forwarder, free the entry — is the one whose judgment
   245	becomes a `:lapsed` fact. Two judges with different tolerances may disagree and
   246	neither is wrong; what settles it is **possession, not truth**, and that is
   247	what reclaiming *without consensus* means. Note what is not claimed here: the
   248	same interpreter both judges and reclaims, so this is not the separation of
   249	interpretation from execution, and it should not be dressed as that invariant.
   250	
   251	**The judge is an interpreter over two streams.** One carries the lease facts.
   252	The other carries **ticks** — plain data appended by a host adapter when a
   253	timer fires, which is the ordinary form the governing document prescribes for
   254	turning a host event into stream data. A judge with a silent holder has nothing
   255	else to wake it: unlike the precedent's VM, it does not reach its points as a
   256	side effect of running, and on a host where nothing runs without an event, a
   257	grantor whose holder has gone quiet would otherwise never judge at all while
   258	its process stays alive. The clock is **read at a tick, never armed by the
   259	judge**; no timer holds a callback into lease code, and the cadence is a
   260	declared property of the composition rather than something inherited free.
   261	
   262	The order of operations at a judgment point is fixed:
   263	
   264	1. **Judge.** Compare the interval against the duration and tolerance.
   265	2. **Reclaim.** Perform the act — close, stop, free — which must be idempotent,
   266	   because it may be retried.
   267	3. **Append `:lapsed`.** The record follows the act.
   268	
   269	The record is therefore evidence that a reclaim was performed, never a promise
   270	that one will be. If the append itself fails, the resource is reclaimed and
   271	unrecorded; the grantor retries the append, and readers must treat the absence
   272	of a `:lapsed` fact as no evidence of continued tenure. This is the same
   273	irreducible corner the WebSocket boundary already documents: a party whose only
   274	channel has failed cannot record its own failure on that channel.
   275	
   276	## Whose clock decides
   277	
   278	**Durations cross boundaries; moments never do.** A grant carries a duration, a
   279	renewal carries nothing, and no expiry timestamp appears in any fact.
   280	
   281	The judge measures intervals between its own observations, so **offset skew
   282	cannot enter the comparison** — it happens entirely on one clock. **Rate skew
   283	remains**: a judge whose clock runs fast relative to the holder's shortens the
   284	real tolerance below what the holder expects, and that is a false lapse with
   285	zero transit delay. The renewal fraction absorbs it, which makes the sizing
   286	relation a skew budget as well as a load budget.
   287	
   288	Transit delay widens the window rather than narrowing it — a renewal is
   289	observed at or after it was appended — so delay errs in the *holder's* favour.
   290	That is not the same as erring safe. It is safe for the holder and unsafe for
   291	the scarce resource: an arbitrarily delayed or late-observed renewal from an
   292	already-dead holder buys a fresh duration, because the evidence carries no
   293	generation or sequence and its arrival is its entire meaning. This document
   294	takes that trade deliberately, preferring to extend a dead tenure over
   295	reclaiming a live one.
   296	
   297	**The hard limit is a partition that outlasts the duration.** It destroys a
   298	live grant whose holder performed its full duty, and no discipline available
   299	here prevents it — a `:dao.stream/ok` on an outbound path implies nothing about
   300	delivery. The holder's remedy is renegotiation when it reconnects.
   301	
   302	Sizing is stated as relations, never numbers, because the numbers belong to
   303	compositions: the medium's retention window exceeds the judge's lag; the
   304	tolerance covers expected flight time and rate skew; the granted duration
   305	exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval.
   306	
   307	## Judging lapse
   308	
   309	The judge keeps, as private state: each lease's **terms** — subject, duration,
   310	cap — and the observation time, on its own clock, of the **most recent relevant
   311	fact**, which is the grant or the newest renewal, whichever is later.
   312	
   313	Seeding from the grant is what makes a never-renewed lease judgeable. A holder
   314	that accepts a grant and dies before its first renewal supplies no renewal to
   315	measure from, and without this rule such a lease could never lapse — the exact
   316	leak the document exists to prevent, in its most likely form.
   317	
   318	**This state is not rebuildable from the stream.** Renewals carry no time, so
   319	observation times are judge-local facts that appear nowhere in the medium.
   320	The terms are worse than volatile: `:accepted` is an ordinary stream element
   321	under ordinary retention, so under bounded retention it can be evicted while
   322	the lease is live, leaving a restarting judge reading renewals for a lease
   323	whose duration and cap it cannot recover. A composition must therefore make the
   324	terms durable — persisted by the judge, or restated by the grantor — or accept
   325	the restart rule below.
   326	
   327	**Absence is evidence only over a window the judge actually observed.** A false
   328	lapse has no deopt, so the discipline is conservative:
   329	
   330	- **A gap is not silence.** After any `:dao.stream/gap`, a lease with no
   331	  renewal in the retained tail is **unknown**, and the one-duration rule below
   332	  applies. Do not infer that a surviving older renewal is the newest: eviction
   333	  order is the medium's declared nature and this contract standardizes none, so
   334	  under a medium that evicts arbitrarily a newer renewal may be gone while an
   335	  older one remains, inflating the measured interval and false-lapsing a live
   336	  holder. A judged medium should declare evict-oldest for the same reason a
   337	  deposit destination must; where it does not, the gap rule above is the only
   338	  safe reading.
   339	- **After losing its state** — a restart, or a gap spanning the watched window
   340	  — a judge knows nothing about silence. The honest state is **unknown**, not
   341	  lapsed: "no renewal was found" and "no renewal was observed" are different
   342	  facts, and collapsing them is how a restart reclaims a live holder's grant.
   343	  The judge observes for one full duration from resumed observation before
   344	  concluding lapse.
   345	- **A restarting grantor may instead re-grant.** For every resource it still
   346	  possesses it appends a fresh unsolicited `:accepted`, and the earlier lease is
   347	  over by that act. This is the clean recovery where terms were not persisted,
   348	  and it needs no history at all.
   349	
   350	Two limits of the promise follow, and both must be stated rather than left to
   351	be discovered. `:dao.lease/max` caps consecutive tenure **within one
   352	uninterrupted judge lifetime**; every restart resets the measurement, so a
   353	judge that restarts more often than one duration can extend a supposedly capped
   354	lease indefinitely. And a grantor whose restarts outpace one duration never
   355	completes an observation window at all, so it leaks continuously while every
   356	component reports healthy.
   357	
   358	## Whose obligation
   359	
   360	A grantor that never judges leaks its resource without bound. That obligation
   361	cannot be a callback and cannot be a component, so it lives in two places.
   362	
   363	**Possession.** The leak lands on the grantor's own resource, so the party with
   364	the incentive is the party with the authority. For a resource that dies with
   365	its process — a socket, an in-memory table — a grantor that stops running holds
   366	nothing anyone can reach, and the obligation ends with it. **This does not hold
   367	for durable resources.** A reserved slot on disk or a claim on a persistent log
   368	outlives its grantor, and a grantor's death then leaves the grant held with no
   369	judge running — precisely the stuck state this document exists to end. A lease
   370	over a durable resource requires a judge whose durability matches the
   371	resource's.
   372	
   373	**Declaration.** A composition that grants leases declares its judgment
   374	cadence and wires the tick stream and judging interpreter that realize it.
   375	Failing to is a defect — but it is honest to say that nothing can refuse it.
   376	The WebSocket boundary can reject a reject-mode deposit destination because a
   377	constructor inspects a destination's declared nature; there is no counterpart
   378	here, because no constructor can inspect whether a composition will later
   379	append an `:accepted` fact. This is a defect detectable by review, with no
   380	assembly-time or runtime signature, and the document claims no enforcement it
   381	does not have.
   382	
   383	What a grantor can promise, it promises about itself: **a grantor judging at
   384	its declared cadence reclaims every lapsed lease within duration plus tolerance
   385	plus cadence, plus the cost of the reclaim itself** — conditional on the judge
   386	running continuously enough to complete an observation window, per the restart
   387	limits above.
   388	
   389	## What a lease may never do
   390	
   391	- **Never a live object.** Everything one can do about a lease is append a fact
   392	  or judge one.
   393	- **Never fires into lease code.** No timer holds a callback and no
   394	  notification marks a lapse; a tick is data on a stream like any other event,
   395	  and a composition that wants to observe reclaims reads the medium like any
   396	  other interpreter.
   397	- **Never a registry.** A shared lease table is hidden global state. The
   398	  judge's private derived view is as far as accounting goes.
   399	- **Never puts absolute time on the wire.**
   400	- **Never erases.** A reclaim frees the resource, not the record; the medium's
   401	  retention policy is the only eraser, here as everywhere.
   402	- **Never renewed by machinery.** A holder renews from its own control flow,
   403	  because renewal is evidence the holder is alive.
   404	- **Never touches retention.** This is the load-bearing denial: a lease may not
   405	  extend retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no DaoStream operation
   406	  may consult a lease. `dao.stream.md` is explicit that eviction never waits on
   407	  a reader, and a lease that could hold a stream open would make one stalled
   408	  holder every other reader's unbounded growth.
   409	
   410	## Renewal forever
   411	
   412	A hostile holder gains little by renewing forever, and what it gains is priced.
   413	Renewal buys tolerance for silence and nothing else. Renewing forever holds the
   414	lease forever, which `:dao.lease/max` bounds within a judge lifetime — past it
   415	the grantor lapses regardless of renewals, and an honest long-lived holder
   416	proposes anew. Renewal costs presence, which is the honest price, and a holder
   417	able to pay it indefinitely is indistinguishable from a legitimately long-lived
   418	one; distinguishing them is admission policy — who is granted anything at all —
   419	never lease vocabulary.
   420	
   421	Whether a leased grant is dangerous is judged where it is granted. A paused
   422	forwarder retains nothing extra, so that pause harms only the pauser; a lease
   423	over a genuinely scarce resource needs its cap. The vocabulary carries the cap;
   424	the composition decides it.
   425	
   426	## Delegated renewal
   427	
   428	A renewal is evidence about its author. A party emitting evidence about
   429	*another* party is delegation, and delegation is **out of scope here** — denied
   430	now rather than deferred vaguely, with the conditions for revisiting stated so
   431	the judgment is auditable.
   432	
   433	An agent that renews on a holder's behalf without holding evidence about that
   434	holder is Jini's `LeaseRenewalManager` in cleaner materials, manufacturing
   435	false liveness for a wedged holder. An agent that renews *on evidence* needs
   436	its own liveness signal from the holder — which is a lease — so the honest
   437	structure is a chain of ordinary leases, each link its own grantor and
   438	possessor, terminating at whoever is doing the work.
   439	
   440	Such a chain is safe but not meaningful by default. The terminal grantor is
   441	never unsafe: it reclaims when its immediate holder goes quiet, and that local
   442	rule is unchanged by anything below it, which it cannot even see. What a chain
   443	changes is what those renewals *mean*, and that is a property of each
   444	intermediate renewer's composition, checkable only by reading it:
   445	
   446	> For each link, the renewer's predicate is *I append a renewal only while I
   447	> hold fresh evidence, observed on my own clock, that my immediate holder is
   448	> still there.* The end-to-end meaning — that the terminal grantor's reclaim
   449	> tracks the worker's death — holds only if that predicate is true at **every**
   450	> link. Any weaker answer is not a weakening but a **cut**: from that point
   451	> outward, renewals prove only the renewer's liveness.
   452	
   453	Neither benefit usually claimed for delegation survives inspection. Batching
   454	amortized a remote call in Jini; here N renewals to one grantor are N appends
   455	the judge already reads in one pass, cross-grantor batching is impossible by
   456	construction, and coalescing frames on a wire is carriage the transport owns.
   457	Cadence decoupling is worse than the alternative: the terminal grantor cannot
   458	reclaim faster than the innermost sub-lease permits, so a chain is strictly
   459	worse than granting the loose duration directly. Each link also adds its own
   460	duration and cadence to detection latency, and a dead manager fells every
   461	sub-holder beneath it.
   462	
   463	What delegation genuinely offers — one accountable counterparty, with admission
   464	and sub-allocation a link down — needs no delegation at all: the manager is
   465	simply **holder of record**. It holds the grantor's lease, sub-leases its own
   466	attention beneath it, and the grantor sees one holder and is told nothing about
   467	the rest. That shape is ordinary composition and is legal today.
   468	
   469	Should a real need for third-party renewal arrive, the weakest sufficient
   470	mechanism is a holder-authored fact designating acceptable renewers, with the
   471	judge counting a renewal only from the holder or a designated renewer. It
   472	presupposes exactly what *Attribution is a composition duty* already requires,
   473	and nothing more. Accepting any renewal that merely names a lease is not a
   474	weaker mechanism but the absence of one — tolerable only where the medium's own
   475	admission already restricts writers, which is designation done implicitly and
   476	should be named as such rather than adopted as a design. Depth beyond one
   477	delegation would need chained, attenuable, offline-verifiable authorization,
   478	which is a different problem and not this document's.
   479	
   480	## Out of scope
   481	
   482	What is leasable is the domain's business; this document standardizes
   483	vocabulary and duties, and refuses all policy.
   484	
   485	**Carriage** — how these facts cross a particular boundary — belongs to the
   486	transport documents. Renewals ride as ordinary payload values, and so does a
   487	`:lapsed` fact: reclamation reaches a remote holder as data, which is the
   488	reading consistent with a transport that never learns lease vocabulary.
   489	
   490	This document deliberately assigns **no close code**. A reclaimed WebSocket
   491	connection surfaces to its client as an ordinary `:ws/closed`, and reattaching
   492	yields a fresh unsolicited grant. The distinguishing close code
   493	`dao.stream.ws.md` defines means something else — the served stream *ended*, so
   494	the client must stop and not reattach — and a reclaimed attachment is not an
   495	ended stream. Where a served stream is shared between attachments, reclaiming
   496	one lease cannot end it, and the transport defines no per-attachment code.
   497	Should reclaim ever need to be distinguishable on the wire, that is an item for
   498	the transport's own deferred close-code design, raised there rather than
   499	settled here.
   500	
   501	`dao.stream.ws.md` also defers liveness probing, and its Deferred list awaits a
   502	durable home for a pause vocabulary. Both are lease-shaped, and this document
   503	is the plausible home for their semantics; neither is claimed here until the
   504	transport's wire-contract gate settles what crosses the wire.
   505	
   506	**Cancellation of in-flight work** is untouched. A lease reclaims what silence
   507	abandons; it cannot retract a request already made, and waiting for a lease to
   508	lapse is the wrong instrument for a one-shot answer that will never arrive.
   509	
   510	**Transfer of a lease between holders**, and **authorization of who may be
   511	granted anything at all**, wait for a real need.
   512	
   513	Three uses justify this vocabulary: the pause a reader holds over a forwarder,
   514	the lifetime of a served connection, and a claim an agent leaves on shared
   515	work. Three is enough for a small vocabulary; it is not a reason to make every
   516	timeout, cancellation, or lock a lease.

