| **High** | [Lines 111–120, 148–180, 307–349](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:111>) | Release exists in the vocabulary but has no operational semantics. The judge’s “most recent relevant fact” excludes it, and nothing says whether a valid release causes immediate reclaim, how it races with lapse, or whether both `:released` and `:lapsed` may exist. Accepted and rejected are likewise both permitted once for the same id. | Define deterministic validity and terminal rules: which author may release; whether release is a request or merely evidence; the required reclaim action; late-event handling; and conflict precedence for accepted/rejected and released/lapsed. If `:released` remains holder-authored, do not describe it as evidence that the grantor actually freed the resource. |
| **High** | [Lines 201–211, 281–300, 313–316](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:201>) | “Stop acting at the bound” is presented as holder safety, but grant seeding begins the judge’s window when it observes the grant, while the holder begins later when the grant reaches it. A delayed grant can therefore leave the holder acting within its local bound after the grantor has reclaimed. Renewal timing does not repair the first window. | Require a holder to emit its first renewal immediately after observing the grant and before acting, with a grantor-side activation rule, or explicitly state that the holder rule is only damage limitation and supplies no exclusion guarantee during the first window. If activation must be knowable to the holder, an acknowledged activation protocol is required. |
