| Severity | Section or line | The claim | Exact correction |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Medium** | [Lines 27–37 versus 262–274](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:27>) | The precedent summary still says guard failure is “recorded as a fact followed by fallback,” directly reversing the normative reclaim-then-record ordering. | Change the summary to: failure causes fallback/reclaim, whose successful performance is then recorded. |
| **Medium** | [Lines 40–67](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:40>) | The promised derivation—two structural divergences, three others derived from the first—does not hold. Stateful observation and absence-over-a-window follow from the remote guard subject; obligatory judgment does not. A local lease would still require judgment. Also, “applying a patch is optional” is too broad: the precedent says an **accepted** patch is applied; choosing not to accept one is what preserves correctness. | Classify obligatory judgment as an independent semantic divergence arising from bounded resource tenure. Say patch **acceptance** is optional, whereas applying an accepted patch is part of the precedent’s stated protocol. Remove the “other three follow from the first” count. |
| **Medium** | [Lines 318–325](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:318>) | Terms may be made durable by being “restated by the grantor,” but the vocabulary allows each status only once and defines no restatement fact. Another `:accepted` either violates that rule or creates the unsafe replacement described later. | Remove restatement as an option, or define an authoritative checkpoint/snapshot fact, including its author, identity, cap origin, ordering, and restart semantics. Persisting the judge’s private terms view is currently the only specified valid option. |
| **Medium** | [Lines 288–300](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:288>) | “Transit delay widens the window” and “the hard limit is a partition that outlasts the duration” are overstated. Delay widens tenure only if the renewal arrives before judgment; otherwise it causes lapse. A partition longer than duration can still survive when tolerance and tick phase cover the excess, while a shorter one can fail depending on when it begins relative to the last renewal. | State the actual condition: continuity is lost when no eligible renewal is observed before the judge’s strict `duration + tolerance` boundary at a completed judgment pass. Treat partition length alone as insufficient to decide the outcome. |
| **Medium** | [Lines 134–140, 350–354](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:134>) | Implementers still must invent the duration representation and cap comparison. “Quantity carrying its unit” does not define its data shape, allowed units, precision, conversion, overflow behavior, or whether `:max` uses the same strict boundary and tolerance as duration. | Specify the canonical plain-data schema and comparison rules, including valid units, normalization, numeric range, strictness, and whether tolerance applies to `:max`. |
| **Medium** | [Lines 363–371](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:363>) | A durable resource merely requires a judge “whose durability matches” it. That does not specify recovery ownership, duplicate judges, fencing, or how a restarted judge acquires authority over the resource. An implementer must invent the most consequential part of durable leasing. | Either restrict this document to process-scoped resources, or define the durable possessor/handoff precondition: exactly which durable authority may reclaim, how concurrent incarnations are fenced, and which persisted state survives handoff. |

No low-severity findings.

The following advertised fixes did land without relocating their defects: ticks are data rather than callbacks; the grant seeds the initial observation window; observation times are explicitly non-rebuildable; reclaim precedes `:lapsed`; offset and rate skew are distinguished; retention remains independent; and lease reclaim correctly remains ordinary `:ws/closed` rather than reusing the served-stream-ended close code.

## What the rewrite made worse

The rewrite introduced three regressions:

- The two-stream tick correction replaced the missing wake-up mechanism with an unspecified cross-stream scheduling race.
- Restart recovery now asserts that a fresh grant silently ends an old lease, creating a more dangerous unrecorded overlap than the earlier merely incomplete restart story.
- The forced “two structural plus three derived” precedent taxonomy now overclaims a derivation that is false and obscures the real independent reason judgment is mandatory.

The conservative wording also overshoots in two places: a partition lasting longer than duration is not the hard boundary, and transit delay is not uniformly holder-favouring.
